1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,808
This repository demonstrates NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Transcribe on a single H one hundred GPU.

2
00:00:06,808 --> 00:00:15,582
Three real inference recordings show timed captions: a two-person conversation, a historical speech, and NASA radio.

3
00:00:15,582 --> 00:00:18,292
NeMo Speech and vLLM serve the model.

4
00:00:18,292 --> 00:00:24,078
Python scripts submit audio, save responses, verify timing, and build captions.

5
00:00:24,078 --> 00:00:27,960
Remotion turns those saved results into short videos.

6
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:31,691
A mono wave file enters the transcription request.

7
00:00:31,691 --> 00:00:37,213
The saved JSON includes text, word times, speaker tags, and an audio hash.

8
00:00:37,213 --> 00:00:40,570
Caption export checks the hash and alignment.

9
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:44,934
The conversation demo runs thirty-four seconds and has six speaker turns.

10
00:00:44,934 --> 00:00:51,509
Its subtitle script groups aligned words by speaker, sentence ending, and pause, making the timeline readable.

11
00:00:51,509 --> 00:00:55,847
Each clip remains tied to the audio and raw response that produced it.

12
00:00:55,847 --> 00:01:02,144
The output is unedited, including errors, so the radio transcript needs listening review before reuse.
